## Why This Is Asked

Tests end-to-end design: IoT ingress, data governance, model integration, privacy controls, and scalable user access governance. It probes architectural decisions, trade-offs, and verification strategies.

## Key Concepts

- Azure IoT Hub ingestion and event-driven processing
- Data Lake Gen2 storage for raw and structured telemetry
- Vector store for embeddings and scheduled refresh cadence
- API Management with per-tenant quotas and private networking to Azure OpenAI
- Purview for data lineage and governance
- Data residency, PII redaction, and security testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js snippet: per-tenant quota check in Azure Functions
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
  // fetch quota from a store (e.g., Cosmos Redis cache)
  const quota = await getQuota(tenant);
  if (quota.remaining <= 0) {
    context.res = { status: 429, body: 'Quota exhausted' };
    return;
  }
  await consumeOne(tenant);
  context.res = { status: 200, body: 'ok' };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test latency under burst IoT traffic while preserving data residency?
- What strategies ensure embeddings stay up-to-date without leaking sensitive telemetry to the model?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests NL2SQL grounding, RBAC enforcement, privacy controls, and explainability in a realistic Azure OpenAI setup.

## Key Concepts
- NL2SQL grounding to a versioned KB
- Tenant-scoped RBAC gating
- PII redaction in both prompts and results
- Explainable AI: SQL + execution plan
- Safe fallback when policies block access

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation sketch: authenticate, fetch KB, generate SQL, enforce guards
async function nl2sql(user, nlQuery) {
  const ctx = await authenticate(user);
  const kb = await fetchKB(ctx tenantId);
  const sql = await generateSQL(nlQuery, kb);
  const guarded = applyRBAC(sql, ctx);
  const redacted = redactPII(guarded, ctx);
  const explain = explainSQL(redacted);
  return { sql: redacted, explain };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for cross-tenant data leakage?
- How would you handle schema drift in the KB and keep responses in sync?

## Why This Is Asked
Explores runtime policy update, data residency, and per-tenant isolation without redeploying models. 

## Key Concepts
- Runtime policy engine integrated with config store
- Per-tenant data sovereignty and RBAC
- Latency targets and fallback paths

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: policy fetch & eval before OpenAI call
async function handle(req, tenantId){
  const policies = await fetchPolicy(tenantId);
  if(!evaluate(policies, req)) throw new Error('Policy violation');
  const redacted = redact(req.body);
  return await callOpenAI(redacted);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test runtime policy changes with live traffic?
- How do you roll back a faulty policy update?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests design for guardrails, data isolation, and cost controls in Azure OpenAI deployments, a common advanced requirement for regulated customers.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic safety policy engine
- Prompt redaction and blocking
- API Management routing
- Grounding with versioned KB (Blob + Cognitive Search)
- Per-tenant isolation (Cosmos DB) and quotas
- Telemetry and auditing (Data Lake)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal policy eval
function evaluatePrompt(prompt, policies) {
  for (const p of policies) {
    if (p.types?.some(t => prompt.includes(t))) {
      return {blocked: true, reason: p.reason}
    }
  }
  // simple redact example
  const redacted = prompt.replace(/SSN-\d{4}/g, '[REDACTED_SSN]')
  return {blocked: false, redacted}
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement per-tenant billing and quotas at API level?
- How would you test guardrails under burst traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes governance at scale: per-tenant safety, data residency, and cost-aware routing in a live Azure OpenAI pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-aware policy packs in blob storage
- PDP (policy decision point) in Azure Functions
- Data residency routing to OpenAI regional endpoints
- Redaction and guardrails before outbound calls
- Auditing and observability via Azure Monitor

## Code Example
```javascript
function evaluatePDP(tenantId, metadata, policies) {
  const policy = policies[tenantId] || policies["default"];
  // residency check
  if (policy.residency && policy.residency !== metadata.region) return { action: "block" };
  // budget constraint
  if (typeof policy.budget === "number" && metadata.cost > policy.budget) return { action: "redirect", destination: "onPrem" };
  // allowed destination
  if (policy.allowedDestinations?.includes("OpenAI")) return { action: "allow", destination: "OpenAI" };
  return { action: "block" };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test PDP edge cases (missing tenant, malformed metadata, policy drift)?
- How would you implement zero-downtime policy updates and per-tenant rollbacks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a privacy-preserving, multi-tenant moderation pipeline across Azure services, emphasizing data residency, policy enforcement, and drift detection. It also probes integration details and governance touchpoints.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation and data residency with regional OpenAI endpoints
- PII redaction prior to model invocation
- Per-tenant quotas and API Management policies
- Data lineage and governance via Purview
- Drift detection using Azure Monitor and a lightweight Azure ML detector
- End-to-end data flow and secure policy binding

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny function wrapper showing data flow
async function processRequest(tenantId, input) {
  // redact PII before OpenAI call
  const redacted = redactPII(input);
  // route to region-specific OpenAI endpoint
  const endpoint = regionForTenant(tenantId);
  return callOpenAI(endpoint, { tenant: tenantId, text: redacted });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate data residency across regions in practice?
- What testing strategies ensure prompt-injection defenses stay effective after policy updates?

## Why This Is Asked
To assess hands-on Azure AI wiring for accessibility use cases, including voice input, multilingual output, and privacy controls, all grounded in a managed KB.

## Key Concepts
- Azure API Management as a gate
- Azure OpenAI with grounding to a versioned KB in Blob via Cognitive Search
- Azure Speech to Text and Text to Speech
- Per-tenant language preferences
- PII redaction and interaction logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example flow tying services together
async function handleQuery(audioBlob, language) {
  const text = await speechToText(audioBlob, language);
  const answer = await openai.chat({ messages: [{ role: 'user', content: text }] });
  const tts = await textToSpeech(answer.choices[0].message.content, language);
  return tts;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model per-tenant language settings in Cosmos DB?
- What are the trade-offs between cloud TTS and on-device synthesis for accessibility tasks?
